from biology_qa_writer import q,write_bank
S='4.2.'
items=[
q(1,'What is a tissue?','A group of similar cells working together to perform a particular function.',S+'1','2023 Q01.1'),
q(1,'Name the reagent used to test for protein.','Biuret reagent.',S+'2.1','2022 Q02.5'),
q(1,'Name the enzyme that digests starch.','Amylase.',S+'2.1','2024 Q03.4'),
q(1,'Which blood vessels supply the heart muscle itself with oxygen?','Coronary arteries.',S+'2.4','2023 Q02.5–02.9; 2024 Q01.1'),
q(1,'Name the cells that control the opening and closing of stomata.','Guard cells.',S+'3.2','2022 Q05.1'),
q(1,'Name the movement of dissolved sugars through phloem.','Translocation.',S+'3.2','2024 Q04.3'),
q(1,'Name the plant tissue containing actively dividing cells at growing tips.','Meristem tissue.',S+'3.1','2023 Q01.2; 2024 Q04.1'),
q(2,'Explain why large food molecules need to be digested before absorption.','- They are **large and insoluble**, so cannot pass through the intestinal wall into the blood.\n- Digestion breaks them into **small, soluble molecules** that can be absorbed.',S+'2.1','2024 Q02.3'),
q(2,'State the products of protein digestion and lipid digestion.','- Protein → **amino acids**.\n- Lipid → **glycerol and fatty acids**.',S+'2.1','2024 Q02.3'),
q(2,'Explain why an enzyme normally acts on only one type of substrate.','- Its **active site has a specific shape**.\n- Only a substrate with a **complementary shape** can bind effectively.',S+'2.1'),
q(2,'Explain why the left ventricle has a thicker muscular wall than the right ventricle.','- It must pump blood around the **whole body**, rather than only to the lungs.\n- Its thicker muscle generates a **higher pressure**.',S+'2.2'),
q(2,'Explain how a stent can improve the oxygen supply to heart muscle.','- It holds a narrowed coronary artery **open**.\n- This increases blood flow, delivering more **oxygen** to the heart muscle.',S+'2.4','2023 Q02.9; 2024 Q01.5'),
q(2,'Give two differences between benign and malignant tumours.','- **Benign** tumours remain within one area; **malignant** tumours invade nearby tissues.\n- Malignant cells can spread in the blood and form **secondary tumours**; benign tumours do not spread this way.',S+'2.7'),
q(2,'Give two differences between the substances and tissues involved in transpiration and translocation.','- The transpiration stream carries **water and mineral ions in xylem**.\n- Translocation carries **dissolved sugars in phloem**.',S+'3.2','2022 Q05.2'),
q(2,'Why does high humidity reduce transpiration, if other conditions stay the same?','- The outside air contains more water vapour.\n- This makes the **water-vapour concentration gradient** between the leaf and air smaller, so diffusion is slower.',S+'3.2','2022 Q05.3'),
q(2,'An amylase reaction takes 80 seconds to remove all the starch. Calculate its relative rate, giving a unit.','**Rate = 1 ÷ time = 1 ÷ 80 = 0.0125 s⁻¹.**',S+'2.1'),
q(3,'Explain why an enzyme reaction speeds up as temperature rises towards the optimum, but slows rapidly above it.','- Below the optimum, particles gain kinetic energy, causing more **successful collisions**.\n- Above the optimum, the enzyme becomes **denatured**: its active site changes shape.\n- The substrate no longer fits, so fewer enzyme–substrate complexes form.',S+'2.1'),
q(3,'Explain three ways in which alveoli are adapted for rapid gas exchange.','- Many alveoli give a **large surface area** for diffusion.\n- Alveolar and capillary walls are **one cell thick**, giving a short diffusion distance.\n- Ventilation and a good blood supply maintain **steep concentration gradients**.',S+'2.2','2022 Q06.5; 2024 Q02.4'),
q(3,'Describe the roles of red blood cells, platelets and plasma.','- Red blood cells carry **oxygen using haemoglobin**.\n- Platelets help blood **clot**, reducing blood loss.\n- Plasma transports blood cells and dissolved substances such as carbon dioxide, urea and glucose.',S+'2.3'),
q(3,'A person has a heart rate of 72 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 75 cm³. Calculate cardiac output in dm³ per minute.','- Output = heart rate × stroke volume.\n- 72 × 75 = **5,400 cm³/min**.\n- 5,400 ÷ 1,000 = **5.4 dm³/min**.',S+'2.2'),
q(3,'Explain how smoking can increase the risk of coronary heart disease and why an association in a small survey is not enough to prove causation.','- Smoking can damage artery linings and increase the risk of **fatty deposits/narrowing**.\n- Reduced coronary blood flow can reduce the **oxygen supply** to heart muscle.\n- Other variables, such as diet and age, may differ between groups; these **confounding factors** must be considered.',S+'2.6','2024 Q01.7–01.8'),
q(3,'Explain why wind usually increases the rate of water loss from a leaf.','- Wind removes **humid air** from around the leaf.\n- This maintains a **steeper water-vapour concentration gradient**.\n- Water vapour therefore diffuses out through stomata faster.',S+'3.2'),
q(3,'Describe three leaf-tissue adaptations and link each to its function.','- Palisade cells contain many **chloroplasts**, absorbing light for photosynthesis.\n- Spongy mesophyll has **air spaces**, allowing gases to diffuse through the leaf.\n- The waxy cuticle reduces **evaporation**, limiting water loss.',S+'3.1','2024 Q04.1'),
q(3,'In a potometer, an air bubble moves 36 mm in 3 minutes. Calculate the movement rate and explain why this is not an exact measurement of transpiration.','- Rate = 36 ÷ 3 = **12 mm/min**.\n- The potometer measures **water uptake**, rather than water vapour lost.\n- Some absorbed water is used in photosynthesis or retained by the plant.',S+'3.2'),
q(4,'Explain how bile helps lipid digestion in the small intestine.','- Bile is **alkaline**, so it neutralises acid arriving from the stomach.\n- This provides suitable pH conditions for intestinal enzymes.\n- It **emulsifies** lipids into smaller droplets.\n- This increases the **surface area** for lipase to act on; bile itself is not an enzyme.',S+'2.1'),
q(4,'Describe the route of blood from the body, through the heart and lungs, and back to the body.','- Body → **vena cava → right atrium → right ventricle**.\n- Right ventricle → **pulmonary artery → lungs**.\n- Lungs → **pulmonary vein → left atrium → left ventricle**.\n- Left ventricle → **aorta → body**. Valves prevent backflow.',S+'2.2','2023 Q02.1–02.2'),
q(4,'Compare arteries, veins and capillaries, linking their structures to their functions.','- Arteries carry blood **away from the heart**; thick muscular and elastic walls cope with high pressure.\n- Veins return blood at lower pressure; their **wide lumen** reduces resistance.\n- Veins have **valves to prevent backflow**.\n- Capillaries have **one-cell-thick walls**, giving a short diffusion distance for exchange with tissues.',S+'2.2'),
q(4,'Explain how water moves from the soil into a root hair and then leaves a leaf.','- Water enters root hairs by **osmosis** through partially permeable membranes.\n- It moves into the **xylem**, which carries water up the plant.\n- Water **evaporates** from mesophyll cell surfaces into air spaces.\n- Water vapour **diffuses out through stomata**; water loss helps pull the transpiration stream upwards.',S+'3.2'),
q(4,'The mean amylase reaction times are 180 s at pH 4, 60 s at pH 7 and 150 s at pH 9. Identify the fastest condition, calculate its relative rate, and explain the effect of pH.','- **pH 7** is fastest because it has the shortest time.\n- Rate = 1 ÷ 60 = **0.0167 s⁻¹** (3 significant figures).\n- Changing pH can change the shape of the **active site**.\n- At unsuitable pH, fewer substrate molecules bind; only the three tested pH values can be compared, so the exact optimum needs more measurements.',S+'2.1'),
q(4,'A survey finds 30 cases of a disease among 600 smokers and 20 among 1,000 non-smokers. Calculate the percentage in each group and evaluate what the comparison shows.','- Smokers: 30 ÷ 600 × 100 = **5%**.\n- Non-smokers: 20 ÷ 1,000 × 100 = **2%**.\n- The proportion is 2.5 times as high in the smoker group, showing an **association**.\n- It does not alone prove causation: age, diet or other factors may differ, and the sample may not represent the population.',S+'2.6'),
q(6,'Describe a safe method to test a crushed food sample for starch, reducing sugar, protein and lipid. Include the positive results.','- Mix the crushed food with distilled water; use **separate samples** and clean equipment for each test.\n- Add **iodine**: a **blue-black** colour shows starch.\n- Add **Benedict’s reagent**, then heat in a hot water bath: a green, yellow, orange or brick-red precipitate shows reducing sugar.\n- Add **Biuret reagent**: **purple** shows protein.\n- Add **Sudan III**, shake gently and allow layers to settle: a **red-stained oil layer** shows lipid.\n- Wear goggles; handle the hot water bath and tubes carefully. Use water as a negative control and known nutrients as positive controls so results can be checked.',S+'2.1','2022 Q02.5; 2024 Q03.1'),
q(6,'Describe how to investigate the effect of pH on the rate at which amylase breaks down starch. Explain how to make the comparison valid.','- Put drops of iodine into separate wells of a spotting tile. Use **buffer solutions** to set several different pH values.\n- Equilibrate starch, amylase and buffer to the same temperature in a **water bath**. Keep enzyme/starch concentrations and volumes constant.\n- Mix measured volumes of starch, buffer and amylase; **start the timer** immediately.\n- Every **30 seconds**, transfer a drop to fresh iodine. Record the first time iodine stays orange-brown: no starch is detected.\n- Use **1/time** to compare relative rates because the starting amount of starch is the same. Repeat at each pH and calculate a mean.\n- Keep sampling intervals identical; shorter intervals improve endpoint resolution. Wear eye protection and avoid contamination between samples.',S+'2.1'),
q(6,'Explain how a blocked coronary artery can cause a heart attack, then compare a stent with statins as treatments for coronary heart disease.','- Fatty deposits **narrow coronary arteries**, reducing blood flow. A blockage can stop oxygen reaching part of the heart muscle.\n- Less **aerobic respiration** means less energy for contraction; prolonged oxygen loss can kill muscle cells and cause a heart attack.\n- A **stent** opens the narrowed artery and can restore blood flow quickly, but insertion is invasive and carries clotting/infection risks.\n- **Statins lower blood cholesterol**, slowing further fatty-deposit build-up and reducing risk; they do not physically reopen a blocked artery.\n- Statins need regular use and can cause side effects. A stent treats a particular narrowing but does not remove the underlying risk factors.\n- Treatment depends on urgency, severity and the patient’s risks; the two may be used together alongside lifestyle changes.',S+'2.4','2023 Q02.8–02.9; 2024 Q01.5'),
q(6,'Explain how a large reduction in pancreatic enzyme secretion could cause poor digestion and difficulty gaining body mass.','- Less **amylase** means less starch is broken into sugars.\n- Less **protease** means fewer amino acids are released from proteins.\n- Less **lipase** means less lipid becomes fatty acids and glycerol.\n- Fewer **small, soluble molecules** are available for absorption in the small intestine.\n- Fewer amino acids can be used to build new proteins for growth and repair; less lipid is available for storage.\n- Less absorbed glucose is available for respiration, so less energy is released for building cells and tissues. These linked effects make gaining mass harder.',S+'2.1','2024 Q02.3'),
q(6,'Describe how to use a potometer to investigate the effect of wind speed on water uptake by a leafy shoot. Include one limitation.','- Cut the shoot **under water** and fit it into a water-filled potometer without unwanted air; make all connections **airtight**. Dry the leaves.\n- Introduce an air bubble into the capillary tube and mark its starting position. Allow the shoot to acclimatise.\n- Use a fan to provide several measured wind speeds, keeping **temperature, light, humidity and leaf area** as constant as possible.\n- Measure bubble distance over a fixed time; calculate **distance/time**. With a known bore radius, volume uptake = **πr² × distance**.\n- Reset the bubble, repeat each condition and calculate a mean; check for leaks and allow adjustment between conditions.\n- The instrument measures **water uptake**, not exact transpiration: some water is used or retained in the plant.',S+'3.2'),
q(6,'Explain how damage to roots and their transport tissues can reduce plant growth.','- Fewer functioning root hairs reduce **water uptake**; water is a reactant in photosynthesis.\n- Reduced **nitrate uptake** limits amino acid and protein production, reducing growth.\n- Reduced **magnesium uptake** limits chlorophyll production, reducing light absorption.\n- Damaged **xylem** carries less water and fewer mineral ions to the leaves.\n- Damaged **phloem** can reduce sugar transport to growing tissues and roots.\n- Less photosynthesis means less glucose for respiration and for making cellulose and other new cell materials, so growth slows.',S+'3.1–4.2.3.2','2022 Q04.3')]
write_bank('02 - Organisation','Organisation',items,'- Digestion and food tests: 2022 Q02.4–02.6; 2024 Q02.3 and Q03.1–03.5.\n- Coronary heart disease: 2023 Q02.5–02.9; 2024 Q01.1–01.8.\n- Exchange surfaces: 2022 Q06.5; 2024 Q02.4.\n- Plant tissues and transport: 2022 Q05.1–05.5; 2023 Q01.2; 2024 Q04.1–04.6.\n\nEach theme above appears in at least two papers in the three-paper sample. The practice wording, contexts and mark allocations are new; exact source links and curriculum checks are retained in internal metadata.')
